Reasoning:

Entity types in the schema. Each row is one ACLED conflict event, so the central entity is the event itself, identified by event_id_cnty (e.g. 'POL1001'). The ontology class for an event is bsm:Action, which carries eventStartDateTime, classificationCode, actionDescriptionText, involvedActor, hasResult, and (via BattlespaceConcept) hasGeographicLocation and sourceIdentifier. Second, actor1 and actor2 are two distinct participant entities per row; bsm:Action has bsm:involvedActor leading to bsm:Actor. The column names say "actor", but the sample values ('Military Forces of Poland (2023-)', 'Unidentified Armed Group (Baltic)') are organised groups, so I type them as bsm:Organisation, a subclass of bsm:Actor, which keeps them valid actors while being more precise. Third, the location block (location, admin1, admin2, admin3, country, region) is a geographic hierarchy; the ontology models bsm:Location with locationName, hasGeometry, and the partOf relation on BattlespaceConcept, so I model the event location plus admin2, admin1, country, and region as chained Location entities rather than folding them into literals. admin3 is empty in every sample record, so keying an entity on it would silently drop data; I leave admin3 unmapped. Fourth, latitude/longitude match bsm:AbsolutePoint (latitudeDecimal/longitudeDecimal) exactly, reached via Location.hasGeometry. Fifth, fatalities is a quantitative outcome of the event; the ontology models this as bsm:ActionEffect (effectQuantityNumber) reached via Action.hasResult, so I create a per-event ActionEffect entity rather than dropping the column.

Near-synonym and qualifier checks. event_date vs eventStartDateTime/eventEndDateTime: the column holds a single date and is silent on start vs end, so this is genuinely ambiguous; I map it to eventStartDateTime as the conventional reading and flag the ambiguity rather than pretending it is certain. location name vs conceptName/locationName: for Location entities I use the more specific bsm:locationName; for actors I use bsm:conceptName since Actor has no namer property of its own. event_id_cnty vs sourceIdentifier/alternateIdentifier: it is the source's own identifier, so bsm:sourceIdentifier, not hasAlternateIdentifier. fatalities vs effectQuantityNumber/effectSeverityCode: it is a count, so effectQuantityNumber; I add a constant effectDescriptionCode of 'Fatalities' to state the effect kind. hasResult vs hasResponse/hasFocus: fatalities are an outcome, so hasResult. inter1/inter2 ('State forces', 'Rebels') are actor category labels, mapped to bsm:typeName on Object (inherited by Actor) with moderate confidence. event_type and sub_event_type are both classifications of the action and both map to bsm:classificationCode as two values; disorder_type is left unmapped to avoid a third near-duplicate.

AlternateIdentifier trap: iso is a single generic numeric code column with no scheme/standard column alongside it, so per the warning about scheme-plus-value classes I do not create bsm:AlternateIdentifier; iso is unmapped. Likewise timestamp alone does not evidence a bsm:Observation entity. assoc_actor_1/2, interaction, civilian_targeting, source, source_scale, year, time_precision, geo_precision, tags, and all population_* columns have no suitable ontology property and are unmapped.

IRI transformation consistency check: Event.involvedActor uses actor_{actor1} and actor_{actor2}, byte-identical to the two Organisation subject expressions. Event.hasGeographicLocation uses loc_{country}_{admin1}_{admin2}_{location}, identical to the event Location subject. Event.hasResult uses effect_{event_id_cnty}, identical to the ActionEffect subject. Location.hasGeometry uses geo_{country}_{admin1}_{admin2}_{location}, identical to the AbsolutePoint subject. The partOf chain uses loc_{country}_{admin1}_{admin2}, loc_{country}_{admin1}, loc_{country}, and loc_{region}, each identical to the corresponding Location subject expression. All expressions avoid admin3 since it is empty in the samples. No transformation keys are emitted where raw column values are used, and no empty objects appear.

